{"content":[{"c":["ATTALIA"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Mediterranean seaport in Asia Minor from which the apostle Paul and Barnabas sailed back to Antioch at the end of Paul’s first missionary journey (",{"href":"?bref=Acts.14.25","r":"Acts.14.25","rawR":"Acts.14.25","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Acts.14.25","value":"Acts.14.25"}],"t":"ref","v":"Acts 14:25"},"). The town was founded by Attalus II Philadelphus, king of the province of Pergamum (159–138 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},"), which was taken by the Romans in 779 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"}," and became a senatorial province in ",{"c":["AD"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"}," 43. In Paul’s time Attalia was part of the province of Pamphylia. Today, though its harbor is shallow, it is still an important Turkish seaport (Antalya)."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Attalia","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Acts 14:25","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Acts.14.25","rawQueryValue":"Acts.14.25","sourceRefExact":"Acts.14.25"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"fde52f6fd4e918cbc63391ce69b34e3b695d3d9dbc81dbd923c2f42c4d349414","sourceItemName":"Attalia","sourceItemOrder":611,"sourceMember":"Articles/A.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Attalia","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Attalia","title":"Attalia","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}